    "id": "purgatory-delay-probability-sweep-arms",
    "parent": "purgatory-rate-to-stale-late-arm",
    "kind": "arm",
    "title": "Sweep purgatory.delay_probability at 0.05/0.30/0.50 as three grid arms",
    "description": "Add three grid arms to the campaign block in scheduler_configs/loop/general_vr.json with overlays {purgatory.delay_probability: 0.05}, {0.30}, {0.50}, beside the existing grid (0.15) and grid-no-purgatory (0) arms. This chunk showed the rate does volumetric work no preference replaces (10.8M delayed deliveries/chunk vs 114k total steer preferences), and 0->0.15 is worth ~40% of depth>=6 per run, but nobody has measured whether 0.15 is near the optimum or merely the first value tried. Read per arm from the runs table and campaign.json: P(depth>=5,6,7) per run, violations, runs per second, and utilStats.deliveryEffects.delayed.{deliveries,acted} against all.{deliveries,acted}. If the depth>=6-per-run curve is monotone up to 0.30 or 0.50, raise the default; if it peaks at 0.15 the knob is tuned and the sweep closes it as a question; if higher rates cost throughput proportionally, report depth>=6 per second alongside per run.",
    "category": "config",
    "buildsOn": [
      "steer-term-factorial-campaign",
      "purgatory"
    ],
    "expectedGain": 8,
    "expectedCost": 1,
    "rationale": "The 0 vs 0.15 contrast is the largest single-knob effect the campaign has measured (~40% of depth>=6 per run) and it is measured at exactly two points. A three-point sweep either finds free depth at a higher rate or converts an undeducible constant into a measured optimum, and it costs one campaign block edit.",
    "generalityArgument": "delay_probability is a per-send holding rate applied to every message regardless of handler, role, or message type; sweeping it names no protocol construct and the resulting value transfers to any spec run under the same scheduler.",
